From: Robert Vokac <robertvokac@robertvokac.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] android: fall back to the APK's assets for a relative
 directory again

SDL_SYS_EnumerateDirectory expands a relative path under the app's internal
storage before opening it, and since e1fa336ac it decides whether opendir()'s
failure may fall back to the asset tree by looking at that expanded path. It
is always absolute by then, so a relative directory that exists only among
the APK's assets ("./Content", TitleLocation.Path's "./" plus a folder) no
longer enumerates at all; only an explicit "assets://" URL still does. The
decision now looks at the caller's path, as it did before, and the asset
lookup still receives the relative part.

Not an upstream commit: SDL main still tests the expanded path (checked
2026-10-26 at 52289a276). Carried by CNA (2026-10-26 dependency audit, C-05).
---
--- a/src/filesystem/posix/SDL_sysfsops.c
+++ b/src/filesystem/posix/SDL_sysfsops.c
@@ -97,7 +97,7 @@
     DIR *dir = opendir(pathwithsep);
     if (!dir) {
 #ifdef SDL_PLATFORM_ANDROID  // Maybe it's an asset... that didn't use an "assets://" URL?
-        if (*pathwithsep != '/') {  // don't fall back to asset tree for absolute paths, in case opendir() failed for other reasons, like opendir("/") returning EACCES.
+        if (*path != '/') {  // don't fall back to asset tree for absolute paths, in case opendir() failed for other reasons, like opendir("/") returning EACCES. The caller's path: pathwithsep is already absolute when a relative path was expanded under internal storage.
             const bool retval = Android_JNI_EnumerateAssetDirectory(pathwithsep + extralen, cb, userdata);
             SDL_free(pathwithsep);
             return retval;
